{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is satisfied.** The {impl} code covers each obligation, and the tests exercise the main cases and the failure paths.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Sizes by kind.** `UsageLog::prepared` in `src/authoring/usage.rs` (lines 142-179) pushes one `InputUsage` per input. Each entry records:\n  - `origin`, which is `mandatory`, `habeas` or `ripgrep`;\n  - `kind`, which is `outline`, `requirements`, `definition` or `builtin`;\n  - `bytes`, the UTF-8 length of the text lines joined by newlines;\n  - `lines`, the number of text lines.\n- **Mandatory and additional inputs.**\n  - `assess_subject` calls `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", …)` (`mod.rs` 465-542), and `followup_sources` does the same for follow-up conversations (572-585).\n  - `converse` calls `usage.prepared(<tool name>, &delivery.inputs)` for each successful tool call (`mod.rs` 856-985). The inputs of `habeas` and `ripgrep` calls therefore get the same record, with their tool name as origin. This includes the terminology definitions those tools deliver alongside source.\n- **Matched declarations.** `components` is the length of the input's `matches` array and defaults to 1 for other inputs. A requirements input therefore records its number of matched declarations.\n- **Unique versus repeated.** `unique` is `self.seen.insert(input.to_string())`, so a repeated read, including one that repeats a mandatory input, is recorded as `unique: false`.\n- **Successful versus failed reads.** `UsageLog::tool` increments `tool_failures`, and `converse` calls `usage.tool(name, true)` when `dispatch` errors. A failed read delivers no inputs, so it is counted as a failure and nothing is recorded as an input.\n- **Prepared versus sent.**\n  - `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on every prepared input.\n  - `end_request` sets `sent_in_request` only for a request that returned a response.\n  - Inputs from a failed or throttled attempt therefore stay unsent until a later request succeeds.\n  - \"Sent\" is approximated as \"the request completed\". That is reasonable and explicit.\n- **File discovery.** `list_files`, `outline` and `references` return `inputs: Vec::new()` in `src/authoring/tools.rs`. `converse` still calls `usage.tool(name, false)` for them, so they count as tool calls with no recorded inputs.\n- **Marking.** `configured_record`, `assess_subject`, `followup_sources`, `converse`, `InputUsage`, `prepared`, `begin_request`, `end_request`, `tool`, `skipped_tool` and `tool_name` all carry `{impl}` markers for this requirement. I found no unmarked code that implements it.\n- **Extra behaviour.** The `builtin` kind is the only addition. It records the size of mandatory built-in text, which is a mandatory input, so it is within the requirement's scope.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` (`usage.rs`) checks:\n  - the `outline` kind;\n  - UTF-8 byte count, using the multibyte `λ`;\n  - line count;\n  - `components == 2` for a requirements input;\n  - unique versus repeated, with `origin: habeas`;\n  - the null → submitted → sent progression;\n  - that no source content is stored.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` covers a failed request leaving `sent_in_request` null until the retry succeeds.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` (`mod.rs`) covers eight failed attempts followed by a success, with `submitted_in_request == 0` and `sent_in_request == 8`.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` covers successful, failed and skipped tools, with `list_files` and `outline` calls counted and no inputs recorded.\n- The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` (`tests/authoring_cli.rs`) checks:\n  - `list_files` counts as a tool call but produces no `list_files` input;\n  - a `ripgrep` input has bytes and lines greater than 0;\n  - a unique `habeas` input is recorded;\n  - a repeated `habeas` read is `unique: false` and sent in request 1;\n  - one failed `habeas` read is recorded as a tool failure;\n  - a mandatory `definition` input has bytes greater than 0;\n  - the usage log contains no source, rationale or credential text.\n\n**Minor gaps**\n\n- The `builtin` kind and the line count of requirements inputs are not asserted directly. Both are simple paths through the same code that the tests do exercise.",
  "references": "dc43fb7d9d6f3d57673af4f00f38c1477c5deda399f79d1bccb7a3770e5c7190",
